Here is an example of a C++ program that uses templates to evaluate an Nth Fibonacci number:
```
#include <iostream>

template <int N>
using fib = int;

template <int N>
constexpr fib<N> fibonacci() {
  if (N == 0) return 0;
  if (N == 1) return 1;
  return fib<N-1>() + fib<N-2>();
}

int main() {
  std::cout << fibonacci<10>() << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```
This program defines a template called `fib` that represents an Nth Fibonacci number, and a function called `fibonacci` that uses recursion to calculate the Nth Fibonacci number. The `main` function then calls the `fibonacci` function with an argument of 10, and the result is printed to the console.

Note that this program uses constant expression (i.e. `constexpr`) to define the `fibonacci` function, which allows the function to be evaluated at compile time, rather than at runtime. This makes the program more efficient, since it avoids the overhead of calling a function repeatedly at runtime.

Sure, here's an example of a coroutine queue manager that could be used in Unity:

```
using System.Collections;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class CoroutineQueueManager {
    private Queue<Coroutine> queue;
    private Coroutine currentCoroutine;

    public CoroutineQueueManager() {
        this.queue = new Queue<Coroutine>();
    }

    public void Enqueue(Coroutine coroutine) {
        this.queue.Enqueue(coroutine);
    }

    public async Task<void> Execute() {
        while (this.queue.Count > 0) {
            this.currentCoroutine = this.queue.Dequeue();
            await this.currentCoroutine.Execute();
        }
    }

    public void Reset() {
        this.queue.Clear();
        this.currentCoroutine = null;
    }
}
```

This `CoroutineQueueManager` class has a `Queue` property that stores the coroutines that need to be executed, and a `currentCoroutine` property that keeps track of the currently executing coroutine. The `Enqueue` method adds a coroutine to the queue, and the `Execute` method retrieves the next coroutine from the queue and waits for it to finish executing before continuing to the next one. The `Reset` method clears the queue and sets the current coroutine to null.
This is a simple example and you can adjust it according to your needs.

