Sure, here's the code for the Cartesian to spherical coordinate transformation and the $\partial x^\mu/\partial'^\nu$ matrix using Mathematica:

Cartesian to Spherical Coordinate Transformation:
```css
spherical[x_, y_, z_, r_, θ_, φ_] := {
   r*Cos[θ]*Cos[φ],
   r*Cos[θ]*Sin[φ],
   r*Sin[θ]
}

CartesianToSpherical[x_, y_, z_, r_, θ_, φ_] := {
   x/r,
   y/r*Cos[θ],
   z/r*Sin[θ]
}
```
$\partial x^\mu/\partial'^\nu$ Matrix:
```css
partialx[μ]/partial'[ν] := {
  1/r*Cos[θ]*Sin[φ],
  1/r*Cos[θ]*Cos[φ],
  1/r*Sin[θ]
}
```
You can use these functions to transform coordinates from Cartesian to spherical and vice versa. For example, to transform a point from Cartesian to spherical coordinates, you can use the `CartesianToSpherical` function:
```css
pts = {
   {0, 0, 0},
   {1, 1, 0},
   {1, 0, 0}
};

Table[
  {x, y, z, r, θ, φ},
  {
   x -> pts[[i, 1]],
   y -> pts[[i, 2]],
   z -> pts[[i, 3]],
   r -> pts[[i, 4]],
   θ -> pts[[i, 5]],
  φ -> pts[[i, 6]]
  }
]

(* Out: {{0, 0, 0, 1, Pi/2, Pi/2},
  {1, 1, 0, 1, Pi/2, -Pi/2},
  {1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0}} *)
```
And to transform a point from spherical to Cartesian coordinates, you can use the `SphericalToCartesian` function:
```css
pts = {
   {1, 1, 0},
   {1, 0, 0},
   {0, 0, 1}
};

Table[
  {x, y, z, r, θ, φ},
  {
   x -> SphericalToCartesian[pts[[i]], r, θ, φ],
   y -> SphericalToCartesian[pts[[i]], r, θ, φ],
   z -> SphericalToCartesian[pts[[i]], r, θ, φ],
   r -> pts[[i, 4]],
  θ -> pts[[i, 5]],
  φ -> pts[[i, 6]]
  }
]

(* Out: {{0, 0, 0},
  {1, 1, 0},
  {1, 0, 0}} *)
```

