1. Create a new Firefox extension and add a background script.
2. In the background script, use the `webRequest` API to listen for requests to example.com.
3. When a request is made, use the `webRequest` API to access the response body.
4. Modify the response body to add a div tag with the text "Hello World" in large red letters, using the `innerHTML` property.
5. Use the `webRequest` API to replace the response body with the modified version.
6. Use the `webBlocking` API to block the request, to ensure that the modified response is not sent to the user.

Here is an example of the code for the background script:

```
// background.js

// Listen for requests to example.com
chrome.webRequest.onBeforeRequest.addListener(function(details) {
  // If the request is to example.com
  if (details.url == "https://example.com") {
    // Get the response body
    var responseBody = details.responseBody;
    // Modify the response body to add a div tag
    responseBody = responseBody.replace(/<body[^>]*>/, '<body $1><div class="lnXdpd">Hello World</div></body>');
    // Replace the response body with the modified version
    details.responseBody = responseBody;
    // Block the request
    details.blocked = true;
    // Prevent the network request from being sent
    details.preventDefault();
  }
}, { urls: ["https://example.com/*"] });
```

Note: This is just an example and may not work as is. It may require some additional work to get it to function properly in a Firefox extension.

