    string response;
    try {
        stringstream ss;
        ss << requestUrl << "&" << postBody;
        string requestResult = ss.str();
        vector<string> urlRequests = split(requestResult, "&");
        httpClient http;
        http.POST(urlRequests[0]);
        stringstream ssResponse;
        ssResponse << http.getResponseBody();
        response = ssResponse.str();
    } catch (const exception& e) {
        cout << "Error: " << e.what() << endl;
    }

    return response;
}

int main() {
    // Get the user's search query from the console
    string query;
    cin >> query;

    // Perform the search
    string searchResult = search(query);

    // Print the search results to the console
    cout << searchResult << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This code uses the `http` library to send a POST request to the Google Search API with the user's search query as a parameter. The API returns the search results as a string, which are then printed to the console. Note that you will need to replace `YOUR_API_KEY` and `YOUR_SEARCH_ENGINE_ID` with your own API key and search engine ID, respectively.

Here's a simple HTML app that measures your reaction time:
```php
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Reaction Time Test</title>
	<script>
		function startTimer() {
			// get the starting time in milliseconds
			var startTime = new Date().getTime();
			// set the interval to 1000 milliseconds (1 second)
			var interval = 1000;
			// set the countdown to 60 seconds
			var countdown = 60;
			// loop until the countdown reaches 0
			while (countdown > 0) {
				// calculate the elapsed time and update the countdown
				var elapsedTime = new Date().getTime() - startTime;
				countdown -= interval;
				// display the elapsed time and countdown on the screen
				document.getElementById("elapsed-time").innerHTML = "Elapsed Time: " + elapsedTime + " ms";
				document.getElementById("countdown").innerHTML = "Countdown: " + countdown + " seconds";
				// pause the timer for 1 second
				requestAnimationFrame(startTimer);
			}
			// reset the countdown to 60 seconds
			countdown = 60;
		}
	</script>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>Reaction Time Test</h1>
	<p>Press the start button to begin the test.</p>
	<button onclick="startTimer()">Start</button>
	<p id="elapsed-time"></p>
	<p id="countdown"></p>
</body>
</html>
```
This app will display a "Start" button that you can click to begin the test. The app will then display your reaction time in milliseconds and a countdown that will notify you when the test will end. Press the start button and the timer will start, you'll have 60 seconds to complete the test.

