Vue 3 introduced a new public API for composing components, called the Composition API. To call a Composition API method outside of a component, you can use the `vm` global object, which is a reference to the Vue instance.

Here is an example:
```php
<template>
  <div>
    <my-component :message="message"></my-component>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import { ref } from 'vue'

export default {
  data() {
    return {
      message: 'Hello World'
    }
  },
  methods: {
    callCompositionApiMethod() {
      this.message = 'Hello from Composition API'
    }
  }
}
</script>
```
In this example, we have a component that uses a ref to store a message. We also have a method called `callCompositionApiMethod` that updates the message. To call this method from outside the component, we can use the `vm` global object:
```php
<template>
  <div>
    <my-component :message="message"></my-component>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import { ref } from 'vue'
import { MyComponent } from './MyComponent.vue'

export default {
  data() {
    return {
      message: 'Hello World'
    }
  },
  components: {
    MyComponent
  }
  methods: {
    callCompositionApiMethod() {
      const vm = this.$root
      vm.callCompositionApiMethod()
    }
  }
}
</script>
```
In this example, we have a root component that has a ref to a message and a method called `callCompositionApiMethod`. We can access this method from outside the component using the `vm` global object.

Note that you need to make sure that you only call Composition API methods that are intended to be used outside the component, and not any methods that are intended to be used inside the component.

Sure, here's an example of a C function that takes a `color` struct and an array of `color` structs, and returns the index of the closest match in the array:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

// Define the color struct
typedef struct color {
    int r;
    int g;
    int b;
} color;

// Function to find the closest color in an array
int find_closest_color(color array[], int num_colors, color target) {
    // Initialize variables for closest color and distance
    int closest_index = -1;
    float closest_distance = 100;

    // Iterate through each color in the array
    for (int i = 0; i < num_colors; i++) {
        // Calculate the distance between the target color and the current color
        float distance = sqrt(pow(target.r - array[i].r, 2) + pow(target.g - array[i].g, 2) + pow(target.b - array[i].b, 2));

        // If the current color is closer than the current closest color, update the variables
        if (distance < closest_distance) {
            closest_index = i;
            closest_distance = distance;
        }
    }

    // Return the index of the closest color
    return closest_index;
}

int main() {
    // Define an array of colors
    color array[7] = {
        {0, 0, 0},
        {255, 0, 0},
        {0, 255, 0},
        {0, 0, 255},
        {255, 255, 0},
        {255, 0, 255},
        {0, 255, 255}
    };

    // Define a target color
    color target = {128, 128, 128};

    // Find the index of the closest color in the array
    int closest_index = find_closest_color(array, 7, target);

    // Print the result
    printf("The closest color to %d, %d, %d is index %d\n", target.r, target.g, target.b, closest_index);

    return 0;
}
```
This function first calculates the distance between the target color and each color in the array, then finds the index of the color with the smallest distance and returns it.
You can change the sqrt to a different mathematical function for a different distance calculation

