Here is an example of how the proxy pattern and the thread-safe singleton pattern could be implemented in Java:

1. Proxy Pattern:
```
public interface Proxy {
  // This is the interface that the proxy will implement
}

public class ProxyImplementation implements Proxy {
  // This is the concrete implementation of the proxy
}

public class RealObject {
  // This is the object that the proxy is representing
}

public class Client {
  public void useProxy() {
    // This is the client code that uses the proxy
  }
}
```
2. Thread-safe Singleton Pattern:
```
public class Singleton {
  private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();

  private Singleton() {}

  public static Singleton getInstance() {
    return INSTANCE;
  }
}

public class Client {
  public void useSingleton() {
    // This is the client code that uses the singleton
  }
}
```
To use the proxy pattern and thread-safe singleton pattern together, you could create a proxy that wraps the singleton instance. For example:
```
public class Proxy {
  private static final Singleton INSTANCE = Singleton.getInstance();

  private Proxy() {}

  public static Proxy getInstance() {
    return INSTANCE;
  }

  // This is the interface that the proxy will implement
  // The methods in this interface will be implemented by the proxy
}

public class Client {
  public void useProxy() {
    Proxy proxy = Proxy.getInstance();
    // This is the client code that uses the proxy
  }
}
```
Note: This is just one example of how the proxy pattern and thread-safe singleton pattern could be implemented in Java. There are many other ways to do it.

