Sure! Here is a basic Java program for a calculator that takes input from the user and performs the calculation until the user enters "exit":
```
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Calculator {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
    System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
    double number = scanner.nextDouble();

    System.out.print("Enter an operation (+, -, *, /): ");
    String operator = scanner.next();

    while (!operator.equals("exit")) {
      System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
      number = scanner.nextDouble();

      switch (operator) {
        case "+":
          result = number + number;
          break;
        case "-":
          result = number - number;
          break;
        case "*":
          result = number * number;
          break;
        case "/":
          result = number / number;
          break;
        default:
          System.out.println("Invalid operator. Please enter either +, -, *, or /.");
      }
    }

    System.out.println("Result: " + result);
  }
}
```
This program uses a `Scanner` object to read input from the user and a `switch` statement to perform the calculation based on the operator entered by the user. It will continue to take input and perform calculations until the user enters "exit". Keep in mind that this is just a basic example and can be improved or expanded upon as needed.

