Here is a simple program in Java that performs the union of two sets:
```
import java.util.*;

public class SetUnion {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Set set1 = new HashSet();
    Set set2 = new HashSet();
    Set result = new HashSet();

    // Add elements to the sets
    set1.add(1);
    set1.add(2);
    set2.add(3);
    set2.add(4);

    // Perform the union
    result.addAll(set1);
    result.addAll(set2);

    // Print the result
    System.out.println(result);
  }
}
```
This program creates two sets `set1` and `set2`, and a third set `result` that will store the union of the two sets. It then adds the elements `1` and `2` to `set1` and the elements `3` and `4` to `set2`. Finally, it adds all the elements of `set1` and `set2` to `result` using the `addAll()` method, and prints the result.

The output of this program will be the set `{1, 2, 3, 4}`, which is the union of the two sets.

