34
```

To calculate the distance transform of an image in C++, you can use the following steps:
1. Create a mask image that will be used to mark the foreground (i.e., the object you want to measure the distance to) in the original image.
2. Apply the mask to the original image to create a binary image where the foreground is represented by 1's and the background is represented by 0's.
3. Calculate the gradient of the binary image using the Sobel operator. The gradient will give you the direction and magnitude of the edges in the image.
4. Calculate the distance transform by taking the absolute value of the gradient and multiplying it by a constant value (e.g., 1.5) to increase the sensitivity of the transform.
Here is an example of how you could implement the distance transform in C++ using the OpenCV library:

```c++
#include <opencv2/opencv.hpp>

using namespace cv;

int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
    // Load the original image
    Mat src = imread("original_image.jpg", IMREAD_GRAYSCALE);

    // Load the mask image
    Mat mask = imread("mask_image.jpg", IMREAD_GRAYSCALE);

    // Create the distance transform image
    Mat dist;
    // Convert the mask to grayscale
    Mat maskGray;
    cvtColor(mask, maskGray, CV_BGR2GRAY);
    // Calculate the gradient x-component
    Mat gradX = grad(maskGray, 0);
    // Calculate the gradient y-component
    Mat gradY = grad(maskGray, 1);
    // Calculate the distance transform
    distanceTransform(gradX, gradY, dist, 1.5);

    // Save the distance transform image
    imwrite("distance_transform.jpg", dist);

    return 0;
}
```

Note that this is just a basic example and can be modified and extended to suit your specific needs.

