Sure! Here is a Java code that calculates the fibonacci numbers:

```typescript
public class Fibonacci {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int n = 10; // number of fibonacci numbers to calculate
    int[] fib = new int[n]; // array to store fibonacci numbers

    // initialize first two numbers in the array
    fib[0] = 0;
    fib[1] = 1;

    // calculate remaining numbers using recursion
    for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
      fib[i] = fib[i - 1] + fib[i - 2];
    }

    // print the array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      System.out.print(fib[i] + " ");
    }
  }
}
```

To use this code, simply run it in a Java compiler and it will calculate the first `n` fibonacci numbers and print them to the console. You can adjust the value of `n` to calculate any number of fibonacci numbers you want.

