Here is an example of Dart code that sorts a list of numbers in reverse:
```
void sortReverse(List<int> numbers) {
  // Create a new list to store the sorted numbers
  List<int> sortedNumbers = numbers.toList();

  // Reverse the order of the elements in the list
  for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length / 2; i++) {
    sortedNumbers[i] = numbers[numbers.length - i - 1];
  }

  // Sort the list in ascending order
  sortedNumbers.sort();

  // Set the list to the sorted list
  numbers = sortedNumbers;
}
```
To use this code, you can call the `sortReverse` function and pass in a list of numbers as a parameter. For example:
```
int numbers = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5];
sortReverse(numbers);
print(numbers); // [1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 5, 6, 9]
```
Keep in mind that this code sorts the list in ascending order, but it will print the elements in reverse order because it starts at the beginning of the list and appends the elements in reverse order, rather than starting at the end and appending the elements in forward order.

