In JavaScript, you can sort an array of objects using the `sort()` method. The `sort()` method sorts the elements of an array in place and returns the sorted array. Here's an example of how to use the `sort()` method to sort an array of objects in ascending order based on a property of each object:
```perl
const fruits = ["apple", "banana", "orange", "grape", "pear"];
fruits.sort(); // sorts the fruits array in ascending order
```
In the example above, we have an array of strings representing different fruits. By default, the `sort()` method sorts the elements in ascending order based on their ASCII value.

If you want to sort the array based on a specific property of each object, you can pass a comparison function as a parameter to the `sort()` method. Here's an example of how to sort an array of objects based on the value of the `name` property:
```css
const fruits = [
  { name: "apple", color: "red" },
  { name: "banana", color: "yellow" },
  { name: "orange", color: "orange" },
  { name: "grape", color: "purple" },
  { name: "pear", color: "white" }
];

fruits.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
console.log(fruits);
```
In the example above, we have an array of objects, each representing a fruit with a `name` and a `color` property. We pass a comparison function to the `sort()` method that compares the `name` property of each object using the `localeCompare()` method, which sorts the values in a case-insensitive, numerical order. The `console.log()` statement at the end of the code logs the sorted array to the console.

