The n-th Fibonacci number is a mathematical sequence where each number is the sum of the previous two numbers. Here's a method to calculate the n-th Fibonacci number in C#:

1. Define a class for the Fibonacci sequence:
```csharp
public class Fibonacci
{
    public int FibonacciNumber { get; set; }

    public Fibonacci(int fibonacciNumber)
    {
        FibonacciNumber = fibonacciNumber;
    }
}
```
2. Create a method that takes the n-th Fibonacci number as an input:
```csharp
public static Fibonacci Fibonacci(int n)
{
    if (n == 0)
    {
        return new Fibonacci(0);
    }
    else if (n == 1)
    {
        return new Fibonacci(1);
    }
    else
    {
        int previousFibonacciNumber = 0;
        int currentFibonacciNumber = 1;
        int result = 0;

        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++)
        {
            result = previousFibonacciNumber + currentFibonacciNumber;
            previousFibonacciNumber = currentFibonacciNumber;
            currentFibonacciNumber = result;
        }

        return new Fibonacci(result);
    }
}
```
3. Test the method with different inputs:
```csharp
int input = 10;
Fibonacci fibonacci = Fibonacci(input);
Console.WriteLine("The n-th Fibonacci number is: " + fibonacci.FibonacciNumber);
```
This code will output: "The n-th Fibonacci number is: 55".

Sure, here's an example of a CUDA kernel that performs bilinear sampling from an image:

```scss
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__global__ void bilinear_sampling(unsigned char *output, int output_width, int output_height,
                                  const unsigned char *input, int input_width, int input_height,
                                  int tex_width, int tex_height, int tex_min, int tex_max) {
    int tx = threadIdx.x;
    int ty = threadIdx.y;
    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + tx;
    int j = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + ty;

    int x = (i / tex_width) * tex_width * 2;
    int y = (j / tex_height) * tex_height * 2;
    int pixel_value = input[i * input_width + j * input_height / 2];

    unsigned char r = pixel_value * tex_max / tex_width;
    unsigned char g = (pixel_value * tex_max / tex_width) * tex_height;
    unsigned char b = (pixel_value * tex_max / tex_width) * tex_height;

    output[i * output_width + j * 2] = r;
    output[i * output_width + j * output_width + 1] = g;
    output[i * output_width + j * output_width + 2] = b;
}
```

This kernel takes an input image represented as 8-bit unsigned char values, and performs bilinear sampling on each pixel to produce a color value. The output image is represented as 24-bit unsigned char values, with each pixel consisting of three RGB values. The kernel uses thread-level parallelism to perform the bilinear sampling operation for each pixel in the image.

