To pass a provider to a dialog in Flutter, you can use a `Context` object to provide the value to the dialog widget. Here is an example of how you can do this:
```
class MyWidget extends StatefulWidget {
  @override
  _MyWidgetState createState() => _MyWidgetState();
}

class _MyWidgetState extends State<MyWidget> {
  String _text = 'Initial text';

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Dialog(
      context: context,
      builder: (BuildContext context) {
        return AlertDialog(
          title: Text('My Dialog'),
          content: Text(context.watch<String>._text),
          actions: [
            TextButton(
              onPressed: () {
                context.watch<String>._text = 'New text';
              },
              child: Text('OK'),
            ),
          ],
        );
      },
    );
  }
}
```
In this example, the `MyWidget` widget is a stateful widget that has a string value `_text`. When the `Dialog` widget is built, the `context` is used to provide the value of `_text` to the `AlertDialog` widget. The `context` is obtained using the `BuildContext.watch` method, which returns a `Stream` that emits the current context.

