Yes, here's an example of a Java helper function that uses reflection to copy all fields annotated with the `` @Copy `` annotation from one object to another:


```java
import java.lang.reflect.Field;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class CopyHelper {
    private CopyHelper() {}

    public static void copyFields(Object source, Object target, Class<?> fromClass, Class<?> toClass) {
        try {
            Field[] sourceFields = source.getClass().getDeclaredFields();
            for (Field sourceField : sourceFields) {
                sourceField.setAccessible(true);
                if (sourceField.getAnnotation(Copy.class) != null) {
                    Object sourceValue = sourceField.get(source);
                    Field targetField = targetClass.getDeclaredField(sourceField.getName());
                    targetField.setAccessible(true);
                    targetField.set(target, sourceValue);
                }
            }
        } catch (IllegalAccessException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Illegal access to field", e);
        }
    }
}

```

This function takes four parameters:

*   `` source ``: the object to be copied from
*   `` target ``: the object to be copied to
*   `` fromClass ``: the class of the source object
*   `` toClass ``: the class of the target object

It first gets an array of all the declared fields of the source class using `` getDeclaredFields() ``. It then iterates over each field and checks if it is annotated with `` @Copy ``. If it is, it gets the value of the field using `` get() ``, gets a field of the same name in the target class using `` getDeclaredField() ``, and sets the value of the target field using `` set() ``.

Note that this function assumes that the source and target classes have a common superclass. If they have a different superclass, you'll need to use different field names to distinguish between the two.

Sure! Here's a simple bubble sorting algorithm:
1. Create an array of items to be sorted.
2. Set two pointers, one starting at the beginning of the array and the other starting at the end.
3. Compare the values of the two pointers. If they are different, swap the values of the pointers.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until the pointers are at the end of the array.
5. At this point, the values in the middle of the array are in sorted order.
6. Repeat steps 2-5 until the end of the array has been reached.
7. The sorted array is now complete.
Here's some sample code to illustrate the algorithm:

```css
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    int i, j, temp;
    for (i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code sorts an array of integers in ascending order.

