To set a cookie in Node.js using the built-in `http` module, you can use the `setHeader` method of the response object. Here's an example code snippet:
```javascript
const http = require('http');

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Set-Cookie', 'name=value');
  res.end();
});
```
In this example, we create a simple HTTP server using the `http` module and define a route for the root URL (`'/'`) of the server. When a client makes a request to this URL, the `get` method of the server is called, and we use the `setHeader` method to set a cookie with the name `name` and value `value`. The `end` method is called to end the response and close the connection with the client.

Note that the `Set-Cookie` header is a specialized header that is used to set cookies in HTTP responses. The format of the header is described in the RFC 6265, and it consists of one or more Set-Cookie header fields, each containing one or more name-value pairs for the cookies being set.

