Here is an example of a program that uses `std::map` and `std::sort` in C++:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
  std::map<int, std::vector<int>> my_map;

  // Populate the map with some data
  my_map[1].push_back(2);
  my_map[1].push_back(3);
  my_map[2].push_back(1);
  my_map[2].push_back(4);
  my_map[3].push_back(2);
  my_map[3].push_back(5);

  // Sort the data in the map by the key
  std::sort(my_map.begin(), my_map.end(), [](const auto& a, const auto& b) {
    return a.first < b.first;
  });

  // Print out the sorted data
  for (const auto& pair : my_map) {
    std::cout << pair.first << ": ";
    for (int i : pair.second) {
      std::cout << i << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
  }

  return 0;
}
```
This program creates a `std::map` called `my_map` with integers as keys and vectors of integers as values. It then populates the map with some data. Finally, it sorts the data in the map by the key using the `std::sort` algorithm and a lambda function as the comparison function. The sorted data is then printed out using a loop to iterate over the map.

