Here is an example of a SwiftUI application that generates a random number and displays it to the user with a label, and also includes a button to regenerate the number to a different random number:
```less
import SwiftUI

struct ContentView: View {
    @State var randomNumber = 0
    @State var showRandomNumber = false

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            if showRandomNumber {
                Text("Random number: \(randomNumber)")
                    .font(.largeTitle)
                    .padding(.horizontal, 20)
                    .padding(.top, 30)
            }
            Button("Generate new number") {
                // Code to generate a new random number
                // and update the randomNumber state variable
                self.randomNumber = Int.random(in: 1...100)
                self.showRandomNumber = true
            }
            .disabled(true) // Disable the button until a random number is generated
        }
        .padding(.horizontal, 20)
        .padding(.top, 30)
    }
}
```
In this example, the `@State` property `randomNumber` is initialized to 0. The `@State` property `showRandomNumber` is used to control whether the random number is displayed to the user. Initially, it is set to `false`, so the random number is not displayed. When the user taps the "Generate new number" button, the code generates a new random number using the `Int.random(in:)` method, and updates the `randomNumber` state variable. The button is then disabled until a new random number is generated.

The `Text` view displays the random number to the user, and uses padding to provide some space around the text. The button uses the `.disabled(true)` modifier to disable it until a new random number is generated.

