Here is an example of an IR model implemented in Java without using third-party libraries:


```javaimport java.util.Stack;

public class IR {
  private Stack<Token> st;

  public IR(Token t) {
    st = new Stack<>();
    st.push(t);
  }

  public void add(Token t) {
    st.push(t);
  }

  public Token pop() {
    return st.pop();
  }

  public Token top() {
    return st.top();
  }

  public int size() {
    return st.size();
  }

  public boolean isEmpty() {
    return st.isEmpty();
  }
}

public class Token {
  public int type;

  public Token(int type) {
    this.type = type;
  }
}

public class Prog {
  public Prog(Token t) {
    IR ir = new IR(t);
  }

  public int eval(int x) {
    return ir.top().type;
  }
}
```

This IR model uses a stack data structure to represent the current expression stack. The IR model has methods to add new tokens to the stack, pop a token from the stack, and access the top of the stack. The IR model also has methods to compute the type of the expression, which is the type of the top token on the stack.

The `` Prog `` class is the main class that contains the IR model and is used to evaluate the IR expression. The `` Prog `` class has a constructor that creates an IR object with the given token, and an `` eval `` method that takes an integer and returns the IR expression as an integer.

The IR model above is a basic implementation and can be extended to handle more complex IR expressions.

