Here's an example Rust program that generates a 16-byte random string:
```rust
use std::rand::Rng;
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};

fn main() {
    let mut rng = rand::thread_rng();
    let now = Instant::now();
    let mut str = String::new();
    for _ in 0..16 {
        let nanos = rng.gen::<u64>() as f32 * 1_000_000_000.0;
        let duration = Duration::from_secs(nanos as f64 / 1_000_000_000.0);
        rng.reseed(&now.elapsed() + duration);
        str.push(rng.gen::<char>());
    }
    println!("{}", str);
}
```
This program uses the `rand` module from the standard library to generate a random 16-byte string. It uses the `thread_rng` function to get a global random number generator, which is initialized with the current time using `Instant::now()`. The program then uses a loop to generate 16 random bytes by reseeding the random number generator after each byte using the elapsed time since the last reseed. The resulting string is then printed to the console using `println!`.

Here is a Unity C# script that makes game objects levitate and settle back onto the ground, repeating the process in a loop:
```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class Levitate : MonoBehaviour
{
    public float floatStrength = 5.0f;
    public float floatSpeed = 5.0f;
    public float settleSpeed = 5.0f;
    public float resetDelay = 1.0f;

    private bool isGrounded = false;

    void Update()
    {
        if (!isGrounded)
        {
            float distanceToGround = transform.position.y;
            if (distanceToGround < 0.0f)
            {
                levitateObject();
            }
            else
            {
                settleObject();
            }
        }
        else
        {
            resetObject();
        }
    }

    void levitateObject()
    {
        transform.Translate(Vector3.up * floatStrength * floatSpeed * Time.deltaTime);
        isGrounded = false;
        yield return new WaitForSeconds(resetDelay);
    }

    void settleObject()
    {
        transform.Translate(Vector3.down * settleSpeed * Time.deltaTime);
        isGrounded = true;
        yield return new WaitForSeconds(resetDelay);
    }

    void resetObject()
    {
        transform.position = transform.position.y = 0.0f;
        isGrounded = false;
    }
}
```
To use this script, attach it to a game object and set the `floatStrength`, `floatSpeed`, `settleSpeed`, `resetDelay`, and `isGrounded` variables to appropriate values for your game. You can then modify the script to control the behavior of the levitating object as needed.

