As an AI language model, I cannot create a thread-safe version of `std::vector`. However, I can explain how to use `std::vector` in a thread-safe manner by using the `std::vector` provided by the C++ standard library.

By default, `std::vector` is not thread-safe. To use it in a multi-threaded environment, you can use a `std::vector` that has been constructed with a thread-safe allocation function, such as `std::vector<T, std::allocator<T> >`. This will ensure that multiple threads can safely access the same `std::vector` without causing data races.

Additionally, you can use the `std::vector::emplace` function, which is a thread-safe variant of the `std::vector::push_back` function. This function creates a new element in the `std::vector` and emplaces it in the appropriate position.

Here is an example of how to use a `std::vector` in a thread-safe manner:
```c
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

std::vector<int> vec;
std::mutex vec_mutex;
std::condition_variable vec_condition;

void thread_function() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(vec_mutex);
    vec_condition.wait(lock, []{ return stop; });
    vec.emplace(vec.size(), 42);
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(thread_function);
    std::thread t2(thread_function);
    stop = true;
    vec_condition.notify_all();
    t1.join();
    t2.join();
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, a `std::vector` is created and a mutex is used to synchronize access to the vector. A condition variable is used to block the threads until the `stop` flag is set. The `emplace` function is used to add a new element to the vector in a thread-safe manner.

