In Java, an iterator is an object that allows you to traverse a collection of elements and access them one at a time. It provides a way to loop through the elements of a collection without exposing the underlying structure of the collection.

Here is an example of an iterator interface in Java:
```typescript
interface Iterator<T> {
    boolean hasNext();
    T next();
}
```
This interface defines two methods: `hasNext()` which returns a boolean indicating whether there are more elements in the collection, and `next()` which returns the next element in the collection.

The `for-each` loop in Java uses an iterator to iterate over the elements of a collection:
```css
for (T element : collection) {
    // do something with element
}
```
The `for-each` loop uses an enhanced for loop to iterate over the elements of a collection. The enhanced for loop requires an iterator to iterate over the collection. The `iterator()` method of the collection is used to get an iterator, and the `next()` method of the iterator is used to get the next element in the collection. The `hasNext()` method is used to check if there are more elements in the collection.

Iterators are commonly used with collections such as lists, sets, and maps in Java. They provide a convenient and efficient way to access and manipulate the elements of a collection.

