}
```
This will print out the details of the transaction with the hash `0xmytransaction`. You can also use the `TransactionByID()` method to parse a transaction by its ID, or use the `TransactionBySha()` method to parse a transaction by its SHA3 hash.

Here's a PowerShell script that queries all remote computers in a domain to see if they have Adobe Reader installed using `Invoke-Command`, and exports the list of computers that have it installed to a CSV file:
```perl
# Define the domain name and the list of computer names to query
$domainName = "example.com"
$computerNames = @("computer1", "computer2", "computer3")

# Create an array of computers to query
$computers = [System.Collections.ArrayList]::EnsureCollection($computerNames)

# Define the folder to export the CSV file to
$csvFolder = "C:\scripts\reader_installs.csv"

# Create an array to store the results
$results = @()

# Loop through each computer and check if Adobe Reader is installed
foreach ($computer in $computers) {
    $results += Invoke-Command -ComputerName $computer -ScriptBlock {
        $adobeReaderInstalled = (Get-Item "HKLM:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall\Adobe Acrobat").GetValue()
        if ($adobeReaderInstalled -eq $null) {
            return $false
        } else {
            return $true
        }
    }
}

# Export the results to a CSV file
$results | Select-Object -Property ComputerName | Export-Csv -Path $csvFolder -NoTypeInformation
```
This script creates an array of computers to query, loops through each computer, and uses `Invoke-Command` to check if Adobe Reader is installed on that computer. The results are stored in an array and then exported to a CSV file at the end. Note that the script uses the `Get-Item` cmdlet to check if Adobe Reader is installed on the computer, and returns `$true` if it is installed and `$false` if it is not installed.

